# The passage is largely exhortation; the one inferential thread is the conditional in S2:
# freed from earthly encrustation, the soul would be seen as it is, and so its nature known.
# S1 and S3 only frame the discussion (S3 closes off the treatment of the soul's present forms)
# and are left out. The split of S2 into two steps is my reading of
# "then you would see her as she is, and know ...".
